                <div data-article-body="true" data-track-component="article body" class="c-article-body">

                    
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Welded and joined connections play a critical role in determining the reliability and service life of aerospace structures, yet they remain among the most failure-prone regions due to complex thermo-mechanical–metallurgical interactions. The increasing adoption of lightweight, high-performance, and multi-material systems has further intensified challenges associated with process selection, defect control, and quality assurance. This review provides a mechanism-oriented synthesis of welding and joining technologies for representative aerospace material systems, including aluminum and Al–Li alloys, titanium alloys, steels and stainless steels, nickel-based superalloys, copper and high thermal conductivity alloys, and hybrid metal–composite structures. Material-specific joinability constraints are analyzed in terms of thermophysical properties, phase transformation behavior, chemical reactivity, and interfacial stability, and their implications for defect formation, residual stress development, and structural integrity are discussed. Major joining routes—fusion welding, solid-state bonding, brazing and diffusion bonding, and adhesive-based methods—are examined from the perspective of underlying physical mechanisms and dominant control variables. On this basis, engineering-oriented frameworks are proposed to map material–process compatibility, assess defect-related risks, and guide process selection under reliability and manufacturability constraints. The review further discusses reliability-driven mitigation strategies and emerging artificial intelligence–enabled approaches for process monitoring, defect prediction, and closed-loop quality control. By integrating materials, processes, and quality assurance within a unified mechanistic framework, this work aims to support robust joining design and verifiable manufacturing for next-generation aerospace structures.</p></div></div></section>

                    

                    
    


                    
                        
                            
